    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and all tests pass. Here's a summary of what I did:\n\n## Change: shared household identifier\n\nWhen a create request (`POST /api/owners`) sets `sharesHousehold: true`, the new owner is allowed to join a household already occupied by a same-last-name, same-address owner, and all members of that household are stamped with the same stable `householdId`, which is now returned on the owner resource.\n\n### What I changed\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `householdId` string to the `Owner` schema. This regenerates `OwnerDto` with the field, and MapStruct maps it automatically by name.\n\n2. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a persisted `householdId` field (`@Column(name = \"household_id\")`) with getter/setter, alongside the existing `customerCode`.\n\n3. **DB schemas** \u2014 added a `household_id` column to the `owners` table in all four dialect schemas (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests, plus h2, mysql, postgres), since `ddl-auto=none` under the test profile means the column must exist.\n\n4. **`HouseholdNormalizer`** \u2014 added a stable `id(lastName, address)` helper that derives the shared id deterministically from the existing normalized household key (SHA-256 \u2192 `H-XXXXXXXXXXXX`). Because it's a pure function of the household key, late joiners recompute the same id rather than needing a lookup.\n\n5. **`AssignHousehold`** (new pipeline function) \u2014 when `sharesHousehold` is true and the new owner joins an existing household, it stamps the built owner and back-fills any existing members lacking the id (re-saving them). A lone owner keeps a null id (the collision path never reaches here \u2014 `EnsureUniqueHousehold` already rejects a non-acknowledged duplicate).\n\n6. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `assignHousehold` into the pipeline between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`, under `govern: [ transaction ]`.\n\nI kept responsibilities separate: `EnsureUniqueHousehold` still owns the allow/reject decision, `AssignHousehold` owns id assignment, and the shared household-key logic stays in `HouseholdNormalizer`.\n\nThe `AcceptanceTest` (`coreJoinersShareHouseholdId`) and the full suite are green.",
